  url = {https://papers.ssrn.com/abstract=4747405},
  urldate = {2024-03-27},
  abstract = {This article investigates temporal matching requirements for green hydrogen. While previous studies found that a more granular matching increases production costs, we focus on cost advantages under hourly matching through technological and geographical diversification. To quantify the resulting portfolio effects, we further develop and apply a linear cost-optimization model. We identify two types of portfolio effects, ``technological'' and ``locational'', and quantify them for different portfolio sizes and types of electricity generation technologies. We findportfolio effects in the range of 3--7\% when aggregating two locations, and up to 21\% for a Germany-wide portfolio. We discuss the implications of our findings in terms of discrimination against small players and for the modeling of temporal matching requirements.},
